Evaluation Datasets & Synthetic Data: Building the QA Bench
Today, learning to solve the ultimate validation bottleneck, and mastering Evaluation Datasets and Synthetic Data Generation—building an automated pipeline that auto-generates hundreds of diverse, high-fidelity test cases directly from my internal system logs to ruthlessly benchmark my AI engine.

The Big Idea:
-When you push a C# web API to production, you run unit tests, integration tests, and performance load scripts to certify it won't break under pressure.
-AI systems require an identical rigor, but you can't test a non-deterministic RAG pipeline with a single test case. If you change a system prompt or upgrade your embedding chunk size (Day 18), you might fix an issue for one specific user query while silently destroying accuracy for fifty others. To ship updates safely, you need a Golden Dataset: a collection of hundreds of diverse test queries mapped to explicit, factually correct baseline ground truths.
-Manually writing 500 high-quality test questions, context blocks, and target answers takes weeks of developer time. Instead, we can build a Synthetic Data Generation pipeline. By feeding our raw, parsed production log chunks (Day 18) into a powerful teacher LLM, we can programmatically generate hundreds of realistic user queries, edge-case variations, and target responses, giving us an instant, high-fidelity QA bench.

The Three Pillars of a Robust Eval Test Case
Every evaluation row in your dataset must capture three core properties:
-The Query: The hypothetical question or prompt a user would ask (including variations like messy phrasing or technical keywords).
-The Context: The exact raw factual data or log snippets the RAG system should retrieve to answer that question.
-The Ground Truth: The mathematically correct, ideal answer synthesized from that specific context block.

The Code:
Here is how to construct an automated synthetic dataset generator in C# that parses your local system chunks and compiles an evaluation suite targeting a standardized JSON test harness:

using Microsoft.Extensions.AI;
using System.Text.Json;

public record EvalTestCase(string TestId, string GeneratedQuery, string AssociatedContext, string GroundTruth);

public class EvaluationHarness
{
    private readonly IChatClient _teacherModel;

    public EvaluationHarness(IChatClient teacherModel)
    {
        _teacherModel = teacherModel;
    }

    public async Task<List<EvalTestCase>> GenerateSyntheticSuiteAsync(List<string> rawDocumentChunks, int testCasesPerChunk = 2)
    {
        var evaluationSuite = new List<EvalTestCase>();
        
        // Enforce strict JSON outputs so the generator formats perfectly to our test model schema
        var options = new ChatOptions 
        { 
            ResponseFormat = ChatResponseFormat.ForJsonSchema<SyntheticBatch>() 
        };

        foreach (var chunk in rawDocumentChunks)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"[Synthetic Generation] -> Processing data chunk to extract QA vectors...");

            string prompt = $"""
            You are an expert QA Engineer. Analyze the following technical system context and generate {testCasesPerChunk} distinct evaluation test cases.
            Each test case must include:
            1. A highly realistic, specific user or developer query that requires this exact context to solve.
            2. An ideal, highly precise 'ground truth' target answer based strictly on the facts present in the text.

            Context:
            {chunk}
            """;

            var response = await _teacherModel.CompleteAsync(prompt, options);
            var batch = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<SyntheticBatch>(response.Message.Text);

            if (batch?.Items == null) continue;

            foreach (var item in batch.Items)
            {
                evaluationSuite.Add(new EvalTestCase(
                    TestId: $"eval-{Guid.NewGuid().ToString()[..8]}",
                    GeneratedQuery: item.Query,
                    AssociatedContext: chunk,
                    GroundTruth: item.GroundTruth
                ));
            }
        }

        return evaluationSuite;
    }
}

Key Takeaways:
Data Diversity Matters: Don't just generate generic questions. A robust evaluation dataset must include short keywords, long-winded conversational sentences, and intentional edge cases (like queries containing slight technical typos) to mimic real-world production traffic accurately.
The Teacher-Student Paradigm: You use your heaviest, most capable model (the "Teacher") to generate the synthetic test library. You then use that library to test and grade your smaller, highly optimized, cost-effective production models (the "Students").
Continuous Integration Integration: Once compiled, this dataset becomes a static file in your codebase. Every time a developer alters a prompt template or tweaks the vector indexing parameters, your CI/CD runner execution pipeline pulls this dataset, triggers the system, and outputs an automated precision scorecard.


public record SyntheticBatch(List<SyntheticItem> Items);
public record SyntheticItem(string Query, string GroundTruth);
